{
 "title": "Test 2 - Question 4",
 "topic_keys": [
  "functions_of_random_variables",
  "cdf_method",
  "monotonic_transformations",
  "dad_method",
  "mixed_random_variables"
 ],
 "blocks": [
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "**Lectures 12–15 · Tutorial 6.** **Question 4 asks:** you know $X$. Find the CDF and PDF (or PMF) of a new random variable $Y=g(X)$. For example, $X\\sim U(-1,1)$ and $Y=X^3$.",
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "52e9fc11-81ed-4bab-a495-7e4b29f79a8b",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "500ec2d7-6fac-497b-b5a0-7e71fe769c5f"
    },
    {
     "source_id": "8fc07b50-0ad8-4dd1-9994-4284ef5b512d",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "655b2b16-b365-41d0-a2b3-9b54ab2ab2a7"
    },
    {
     "source_id": "02aff1ce-de48-4c04-b61d-906e7ad59715",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "c95de292-0b58-4163-9f5f-ae7296874275"
    },
    {
     "source_id": "8b50801c-193d-4e1e-92f4-d54dc3a7332b",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "35f99245-aa41-4957-9ed0-1ca171c35e49"
    }
   ],
   "id": "62a9d509-7f7c-5615-a1af-180dd5e78f4b"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 1,
   "text": "Topic 1: what type is Y, and discrete Y",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "662b590e-dd5c-5662-b644-adbd4eaef509"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "**First, look at $X$ and $g$.** They tell you what kind of answer to give:",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "1101d34c-f359-5f9c-995e-2d02b3d4fc45"
  },
  {
   "type": "table",
   "columns": [
    "When",
    "$Y$ is",
    "Answer with",
    "Example"
   ],
   "rows": [
    [
     "$X$ discrete, or $g$ a staircase",
     "**discrete**",
     "a PMF (this topic)",
     "$X^2$ of a die"
    ],
    [
     "$X$ continuous, $g$ never flat",
     "**continuous**",
     "a CDF and a PDF (Topic 2)",
     "$X^3$, $e^X$"
    ],
    [
     "$X$ continuous, $g$ flat somewhere",
     "**mixed**",
     "a CDF with a jump (Extras)",
     "$\\max\\{X,0\\}$"
    ]
   ],
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "8fc07b50-0ad8-4dd1-9994-4284ef5b512d",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "e753d065-56e9-4ae9-89b4-8321aa886a13"
    }
   ],
   "id": "d6566867-4cea-59cc-8fd4-580131da00d1"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "**Discrete $Y$:** push each value of $X$ through $g$. If several $x$ land on the same $y$, **add** their chances:",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "19902c7d-a5e8-5e20-8dc0-91a755ef9f7a"
  },
  {
   "type": "equation",
   "latex": "p_Y(y)=\\sum_{x:\\ g(x)=y}p_X(x)",
   "display": true,
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "52e9fc11-81ed-4bab-a495-7e4b29f79a8b",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "500ec2d7-6fac-497b-b5a0-7e71fe769c5f"
    }
   ],
   "id": "8889ddd6-f1d6-537f-bdaf-b9e7e13b2dbb"
  },
  {
   "type": "list",
   "items": [
    "$p_X(x)$ = the chance $X$ takes the value $x$.",
    "The sum runs over every $x$ that $g$ sends to this $y$."
   ],
   "ordered": false,
   "citations": [],
   "id": "ff9dfb7d-2458-5abd-993f-2684b7fcb8ba"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "Why it works: $Y=y$ happens when $X$ is **any** of those $x$. They can’t happen together, so their chances add.",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "f571cb19-5b6b-5692-a8cc-379e73b3b1c2"
  },
  {
   "type": "worked_example",
   "title": "Example 1 · Two values land on one: $Y=X^2$",
   "prompt": "$X$ is equally likely to be $-2,-1,0,1,2$. Find the PMF of $Y=X^2$.",
   "steps": [
    {
     "explanation": "**Spot:** $X$ is discrete, so $Y$ is discrete. Answer with a PMF.",
     "latex": ""
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**List** where each $x$ goes. Each $x$ has chance $\\tfrac15$.",
     "latex": "(-2,-1,0,1,2)\\ \\longrightarrow\\ (4,\\,1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,4)"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**Add** the chances that land on the same $y$: $\\pm1$ both give $1$, $\\pm2$ both give $4$.",
     "latex": "p_Y(0)=\\frac15,\\qquad p_Y(1)=\\frac15+\\frac15=\\frac25,\\qquad p_Y(4)=\\frac25"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**Check:** they add to 1.",
     "latex": "\\frac15+\\frac25+\\frac25=1"
    }
   ],
   "answer": "$p_Y(0)=\\tfrac15$, $p_Y(1)=\\tfrac25$, $p_Y(4)=\\tfrac25$.",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "f66629cb-c7f1-5635-a2cf-5c4a80641fb7"
  },
  {
   "type": "worked_example",
   "title": "Example 2 · A staircase $g$: a 3-level quantizer (like Lecture 13)",
   "prompt": "$X\\sim U(-1,1)$. $Y=-1$ if $X<-0.5$, $Y=0$ if $-0.5\\le X\\le0.5$, $Y=1$ if $X>0.5$. Find the PMF of $Y$.",
   "steps": [
    {
     "explanation": "**Spot:** $X$ is continuous, but $g$ is a staircase with 3 outputs. So $Y$ is **discrete**.",
     "latex": "Y\\in\\{-1,\\,0,\\,1\\}"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "Each value of $Y$ gets the chance of its $x$-interval. For $U(-1,1)$ that’s length × height $\\tfrac12$.",
     "latex": "\\begin{gathered}P(Y=-1)=P(X<-0.5)=0.5\\cdot\\frac12=\\frac14 \\\\ P(Y=0)=P(-0.5\\le X\\le0.5)=1\\cdot\\frac12=\\frac12 \\\\ P(Y=1)=P(X>0.5)=\\frac14\\end{gathered}"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**Check:** they add to 1.",
     "latex": "\\frac14+\\frac12+\\frac14=1"
    }
   ],
   "answer": "$p_Y(-1)=\\tfrac14$, $p_Y(0)=\\tfrac12$, $p_Y(1)=\\tfrac14$.",
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "52e9fc11-81ed-4bab-a495-7e4b29f79a8b",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "2e08d2f4-7ce3-47dd-8074-24db399beabf"
    }
   ],
   "id": "9b2f2681-99e9-5cea-a01d-c72e1e6dd763"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 1,
   "text": "Topic 2: continuous Y, the CDF method (one method for every g)",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "47f8388c-75ee-5702-b0f1-90b1e42be957"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "**The idea:** you don’t know $Y$’s CDF, but you know $X$’s. So turn the question about $Y$ into a question about $X$:",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "82393eb0-f26e-596a-9ffc-30fcd5ebf846"
  },
  {
   "type": "equation",
   "latex": "F_Y(y)=P(Y\\le y)=P\\big(g(X)\\le y\\big)",
   "display": true,
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "8fc07b50-0ad8-4dd1-9994-4284ef5b512d",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "655b2b16-b365-41d0-a2b3-9b54ab2ab2a7"
    }
   ],
   "id": "745088b8-477e-56d4-8cad-e73cce285674"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "Then the PDF is the slope of the CDF:",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "046f40c4-75e0-5986-824a-199db3127726"
  },
  {
   "type": "equation",
   "latex": "f_Y(y)=\\frac{d}{dy}F_Y(y)",
   "display": true,
   "citations": [],
   "id": "283c9691-64dc-53cc-993f-5bb3eaa1e0d2"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "**Steps** (the same for every $g$):",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "08d5d05b-ebad-5d73-b531-f3532b7997ff"
  },
  {
   "type": "list",
   "items": [
    "**Range:** where can $Y$ land? Push the ends of $X$’s range through $g$. Below the range $F_Y=0$, above it $F_Y=1$.",
    "**Rewrite:** solve “$g(X)\\le y$” for $X$, so it says something about $X$ alone.",
    "**Accumulate:** find $X$’s chance of that, using $F_X$ (or the area under $f_X$).",
    "**Differentiate:** $f_Y(y)=F_Y^{\\prime}(y)$."
   ],
   "ordered": true,
   "citations": [],
   "id": "fe567c57-dae8-5a04-b66e-ec3f0c99cb92"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "**Only the Rewrite step changes**, and it depends on the shape of $g$:",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "f9e891ff-10e8-5c3c-b027-c28a69570387"
  },
  {
   "type": "table",
   "columns": [
    "$g$",
    "Example",
    "$g(X)\\le y$ becomes",
    "so $F_Y(y)=$"
   ],
   "rows": [
    [
     "only goes up",
     "$X^3$, $e^X$, $2X-3$",
     "$X\\le g^{-1}(y)$",
     "$F_X\\big(g^{-1}(y)\\big)$"
    ],
    [
     "only goes down",
     "$-3X+2$, $-\\ln X$",
     "$X\\ge g^{-1}(y)$ (flipped)",
     "$1-F_X\\big(g^{-1}(y)\\big)$"
    ],
    [
     "turns around",
     "$X^2$, $|X|$, $\\cos X$",
     "an interval, e.g. $-\\sqrt y\\le X\\le\\sqrt y$",
     "$F_X(\\sqrt y)-F_X(-\\sqrt y)$"
    ]
   ],
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "8fc07b50-0ad8-4dd1-9994-4284ef5b512d",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "655b2b16-b365-41d0-a2b3-9b54ab2ab2a7"
    },
    {
     "source_id": "02aff1ce-de48-4c04-b61d-906e7ad59715",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "cbcba062-3a3f-4433-ad53-7050ac630d53"
    },
    {
     "source_id": "02aff1ce-de48-4c04-b61d-906e7ad59715",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "c95de292-0b58-4163-9f5f-ae7296874275"
    }
   ],
   "id": "b091a420-ec39-5b45-8153-7e8498de3966"
  },
  {
   "type": "list",
   "items": [
    "$g^{-1}(y)$ = solve $y=g(x)$ for $x$. Example: $y=x^3$ gives $g^{-1}(y)=\\sqrt[3]{y}$.",
    "**Why the flip:** a decreasing $g$ reverses the order. Bigger $x$ gives smaller $y$. Example: $-3X+2\\le y$, divide by $-3$: $X\\ge\\frac{2-y}{3}$.",
    "**Why an interval:** when $g$ turns around, two $x$ give the same $y$ (for $x^2$: $\\pm0.5$ both give $0.25$). So $g(X)\\le y$ holds on a whole stretch of $x$. The lecture calls this DAD (Distribution, Accumulation, Differentiation): it’s the same steps."
   ],
   "ordered": false,
   "citations": [],
   "id": "83307435-4014-52c5-8c9c-60ac433c0db0"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "**Turns around: finding the $x$-set.** Drag $y$. The shaded $x$-set is where the curve is below the line.\n\n```graph\ntitle \"$Y=X^2$, $X\\sim U(-1,1)$: drag $y$\"\nx -1.3 1.3 label=\"$x$\" height=250\ny -0.25 1.25 label=\"$x^2$\"\nslider h 0 1.2 0.36 step=0.01 label=\"$y$\"\ncurve \"x^2\" from=-1 to=1 color=1 label=\"$g(x)=x^2$\"\nhline \"h\" color=2\nregion \"(x*x<=h)*(x>=-1)*(x<=1)*(y>-0.2)*(y<-0.06)\" color=4 label=\"$x$-set: $-\\sqrt y\\le x\\le\\sqrt y$\"\nreadout \"$F_Y(y)$ = shaded length × $\\tfrac12$\" \"min(sqrt(h),1)\"\n```",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "4165a47a-4024-5926-81c5-69796116ac23"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "**Special case $Y=aX+b$** (Lecture 15). It’s a line, so it only goes up ($a>0$) or only down ($a<0$):",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "27c7986b-e15e-584d-bd5b-77d576dc44ad"
  },
  {
   "type": "equation",
   "latex": "\\begin{gathered}a>0:\\quad F_Y(y)=F_X\\Big(\\frac{y-b}{a}\\Big)\\\\ a<0:\\quad F_Y(y)=1-F_X\\Big(\\frac{y-b}{a}\\Big)\\\\ a=0:\\quad Y=b\\text{ always}\\end{gathered}",
   "display": true,
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "02aff1ce-de48-4c04-b61d-906e7ad59715",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "28df4f3d-382b-4027-b8f5-4cc69b31d12d"
    }
   ],
   "id": "efbcc12c-4b06-5d94-af1b-3b03cf8eb4eb"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "text": "**CDFs you’ll plug in** (from Question 2), and the chain rule for the Differentiate step:",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "2f869f0f-9c09-5d57-82ed-bb99912876df"
  },
  {
   "type": "list",
   "items": [
    "Uniform $U(a,b)$: $\\;F_X(x)=\\dfrac{x-a}{b-a}$ for $a\\le x\\le b$.",
    "Exponential$(\\lambda)$: $\\;F_X(x)=1-e^{-\\lambda x}$ for $x\\ge0$, so $P(X>x)=e^{-\\lambda x}$.",
    "Gaussian $N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$: $\\;F_X(x)=\\Phi\\Big(\\dfrac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma}\\Big)$, and $\\Phi(-z)=1-\\Phi(z)$.",
    "Chain rule: $\\;\\dfrac{d}{dy}F_X\\big(h(y)\\big)=f_X\\big(h(y)\\big)\\,h^{\\prime}(y)$."
   ],
   "ordered": false,
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "eaf319c5-b947-4be8-b90d-07e4ea4af2c2",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "c05b6c21-3c16-4ded-81ef-37cac1e34a5b"
    }
   ],
   "id": "3e029acf-9ea8-517e-911d-56fadc0a0730"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "tone": "warning",
   "text": "**Flip the $\\le$** whenever you divide by a negative number or undo a decreasing function. Forgetting it gives $F_X(\\dots)$ instead of $1-F_X(\\dots)$.",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "9201a0c3-faf9-5cd7-9c06-ed4bf29e1c60"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "tone": "warning",
   "text": "**Keep only $x$ that $X$ can take.** $X\\sim U(-1,2)$, $Y=X^2$, $y=4$: the $x$-set $[-2,2]$ gets cut down to $[-1,2]$.",
   "citations": [],
   "id": "f9849797-d433-590c-b532-387c46a80a2a"
  },
  {
   "type": "worked_example",
   "title": "Example 3 · Goes up: $Y=X^3$ (Lecture 14)",
   "prompt": "$X\\sim U(-1,1)$. Find the CDF and PDF of $Y=X^3$.",
   "steps": [
    {
     "explanation": "**Spot:** $x^3$ only goes up, and $X$ is continuous. Use the first row of the table.",
     "latex": "F_Y(y)=F_X\\big(g^{-1}(y)\\big)"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**Range:** $(-1)^3=-1$ and $1^3=1$.",
     "latex": "-1\\le Y\\le1"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**Rewrite:** take the cube root of both sides.",
     "latex": "X^3\\le y\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad X\\le\\sqrt[3]{y}"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**Accumulate:** $X$’s CDF is $F_X(x)=\\tfrac{x+1}{2}$ on $[-1,1]$. Put $\\sqrt[3]{y}$ in for $x$.",
     "latex": "F_Y(y)=\\begin{cases}0,&y<-1\\\\ \\dfrac{\\sqrt[3]{y}+1}{2},&-1\\le y\\le1\\\\ 1,&y>1\\end{cases}"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**Differentiate:** the derivative of $y^{1/3}$ is $\\tfrac13y^{-2/3}$.",
     "latex": "f_Y(y)=\\frac12\\cdot\\frac13\\,y^{-2/3}=\\frac{1}{6\\,y^{2/3}},\\qquad -1<y<1"
    }
   ],
   "answer": "$F_Y(y)=\\dfrac{\\sqrt[3]{y}+1}{2}$ on $[-1,1]$; $f_Y(y)=\\dfrac{1}{6\\,y^{2/3}}$ on $(-1,1)$.",
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "8fc07b50-0ad8-4dd1-9994-4284ef5b512d",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "f45a8bd3-2dcf-40e3-92d0-a724966c6dd7"
    },
    {
     "source_id": "8fc07b50-0ad8-4dd1-9994-4284ef5b512d",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "6db5e3e1-b721-4a08-8829-211016f210f6"
    }
   ],
   "id": "567e240d-9d76-5d06-9e23-ccf9aefaee2b"
  },
  {
   "type": "worked_example",
   "title": "Example 4 · Goes up, with the chain rule: $Y=\\ln X$ (Tutorial 6 Q2)",
   "prompt": "$X\\sim$ Exp$(1)$. Find the PDF of $Y=\\ln X$.",
   "steps": [
    {
     "explanation": "**Spot:** $\\ln x$ only goes up.",
     "latex": "F_Y(y)=F_X\\big(g^{-1}(y)\\big)"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**Range:** $X$ is anywhere in $(0,\\infty)$, so $\\ln X$ can be any real number.",
     "latex": "-\\infty<Y<\\infty"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**Rewrite:** undo the $\\ln$.",
     "latex": "\\ln X\\le y\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad X\\le e^{y}"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**Accumulate:** plug $e^y$ into $F_X(x)=1-e^{-x}$.",
     "latex": "F_Y(y)=1-e^{-e^{y}}"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**Differentiate:** chain rule. The inside, $-e^{y}$, has derivative $-e^{y}$.",
     "latex": "f_Y(y)=e^{y}\\,e^{-e^{y}}"
    }
   ],
   "answer": "$f_Y(y)=e^{y}\\,e^{-e^{y}}$ for all real $y$.",
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "8b50801c-193d-4e1e-92f4-d54dc3a7332b",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "35f99245-aa41-4957-9ed0-1ca171c35e49"
    }
   ],
   "id": "a58703c5-eca4-5637-badd-478cfd18a644"
  },
  {
   "type": "worked_example",
   "title": "Example 5 · Goes down: $Y=-3X+2$ (Lecture 15)",
   "prompt": "$X\\sim U(0,1)$. Find the CDF and PDF of $Y=-3X+2$.",
   "steps": [
    {
     "explanation": "**Spot:** the slope is $-3$, so $g$ only goes down. Use the second row: the $\\le$ flips.",
     "latex": "F_Y(y)=1-F_X\\big(g^{-1}(y)\\big)"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**Range:** $x=0$ gives $2$, $x=1$ gives $-1$.",
     "latex": "-1\\le Y\\le2"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**Rewrite:** subtract 2, then divide by $-3$. That flips the $\\le$.",
     "latex": "-3X+2\\le y\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad X\\ge\\frac{2-y}{3}"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**Accumulate:** $F_X(x)=x$, so $P(X\\ge t)=1-t$.",
     "latex": "F_Y(y)=1-\\frac{2-y}{3}=\\frac{y+1}{3},\\qquad -1\\le y\\le2"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**Differentiate.**",
     "latex": "f_Y(y)=\\frac13,\\qquad -1<y<2"
    }
   ],
   "answer": "$f_Y(y)=\\tfrac13$ on $(-1,2)$, so $Y\\sim U(-1,2)$.",
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "02aff1ce-de48-4c04-b61d-906e7ad59715",
     "version": 1,
     "chunk_id": "28df4f3d-382b-4027-b8f5-4cc69b31d12d"
    }
   ],
   "id": "980a4ef5-56c8-54c3-9489-0d7d2d3a2f97"
  },
  {
   "type": "worked_example",
   "title": "Example 6 · Turns around: $Y=X^2$ (Lecture 15)",
   "prompt": "$X\\sim U(-1,1)$. Find the CDF and PDF of $Y=X^2$.",
   "steps": [
    {
     "explanation": "**Spot:** $x^2$ turns around at $0$. Use the third row: an interval.",
     "latex": "F_Y(y)=F_X(\\sqrt y)-F_X(-\\sqrt y)"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**Range:** $X$ is in $[-1,1]$, so $X^2$ is in $[0,1]$.",
     "latex": "0\\le Y\\le1"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**Rewrite:** a square is at most $y$ when $x$ is within $\\sqrt y$ of $0$.",
     "latex": "X^2\\le y\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad -\\sqrt y\\le X\\le\\sqrt y"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**Accumulate:** that interval has length $2\\sqrt y$, and the height of $f_X$ is $\\tfrac12$.",
     "latex": "F_Y(y)=2\\sqrt y\\cdot\\frac12=\\sqrt y,\\qquad 0\\le y\\le1"
    },
    {
     "explanation": "**Differentiate.**",
     "latex": "f_Y(y)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt y},\\qquad 0<y<1"
    }
   ],
   "answer": "$F_Y(y)=\\sqrt y$ on $[0,1]$ ($0$ below, $1$ above); $f_Y(y)=\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt y}$ on $(0,1)$.",
   "citations": [
    {
     "source_id": "02aff1ce-de48-4c04-b61d-906e7ad59715",
     "version": 1,
